1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Nine STL files. Twelve printed pieces.
R2-D2 | 317 mm body | three powered feet | six drive wheels per pair | Raspberry Pi 4 and KB2040 | 12 V 7 Ah battery

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:25,000
01 | Three feet, six motors, twelve wheels
Load two Adafruit 3777 motors and four 3766 wheels into each foot through the sole, then fit the motor tab bolts. Right and centre feet are shown cut away.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:42,000
02 | Outer legs, two prints each
Slide the lower leg tongue into the foot slot, splice the upper leg over it, run two M8 rods the full length, then fit the ankle pivot and lock bolts.

4
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:55,000
03 | Centre leg and caster
Press two bearings onto the centre-foot stem, drop the centre leg over them, fit the M12 caster bolt and the sixty degree stop pin. The foot then swivels freely.

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:10,000
04 | Lower ring, battery and power
Lower the body floor onto the centre-leg flange bolts. Strap the 12 V 7 Ah battery to its shelf, then fit the speaker, the charge port and the main switch.

6
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:23,000
05 | Upper ring and shoulders
Seat the upper ring. Set both shoulder index pins to the three-leg angle, then run the M12 shoulder bolts through the leg discs into the body bosses.

7
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:35,000
06 | Integral electronics deck
Mount the Raspberry Pi 4, the KB2040, four DRV8833 drivers, both regulators, the ADS1115 and the fuse blocks on the deck printed into the upper ring.

8
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:45,000
07 | Join the two rings
The locating lip aligns the rings. Fit eight M4 seam bolts through the internal flange, then four M8 rods on radius 138 to clamp the whole stack.

9
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:57,000
08 | Head friction drive
Bolt the drive base under the top plate. Fit the seventh motor and its wheel on the pivoting arm, then the spring, the M5 adjuster and the thumb nut.

10
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:08,000
09 | Dome displays and lights
On the bench: the twelve-wire slip ring at the hub, the round TFT behind the radar eye, four logic matrices, two PSI jewels and three holoprojector LEDs.

11
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:21,000
10 | Lazy susan and dome
Screw the lazy susan to the top plate, lower the dome onto it, then tighten the thumb nut until the drive wheel presses the dome plate by 0.6 mm.

12
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:29,000
11 | Power on
Simulated: the logic displays, the holoprojectors and both PSI lights come up behind the dome cutouts, and the dome begins to rotate.

13
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:44,000
12 | Simulated drive
Forward 400 mm, a wide left arc with the centre foot castering to the path, stop, reverse 150 mm, stop, then nudge the dome left and right.

14
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Assembly complete | Prototype design
723 mm tall | 317 mm body | nine STL files | twelve printed pieces | physical fit, strength and driving tests are still required. See the assembly manual.
